diff --git a/debian/README.native-displayid b/debian/README.native-displayid
index 4b9f84a..7455a70 100644
--- a/debian/README.native-displayid
+++ b/debian/README.native-displayid
@@ -1,60 +1,43 @@
-Local native DisplayID HDR detection backport
+Local parsed DisplayID native capability API
 ===========================================
 
-This local package improves libdisplay-info 0.3 detection. It does not by
-itself enable desktop HDR on the ROG panel. Mutter 51.0 additionally requires
-CTA Static Metadata Type 1, and the installed i915 driver reports that its
-Intel HDR backlight interface is unused because static metadata is missing.
-No compositor, kernel, boot setting or panel firmware is changed here.
-
-The 0.3 public headers, exported symbols, struct layouts and SONAME .so.3 are
-preserved. There are no new public entry points or standalone DisplayID APIs.
-The parser privately owns DisplayID v2 blocks and nested CTA children and
-releases partly initialized state when an allocation fails.
-
-For valid descriptions, nested CTA joins ordinary CTA in extension/block
-order. The first match for each relevant CTA domain remains authoritative.
-This deliberately extends 0.3, which previously ignored nested CTA. A mixed
-input with an earlier nested declaration can consequently differ from the
-old ordinary-only result. Native interface features contribute only when
-neither CTA HDR static metadata nor CTA colorimetry exists, and one validated
-block proves RGB encoding plus a complete BT.2020/SMPTE ST 2084 tuple.
-
-This intentionally broadens the existing high-level EOTF getter's derivation:
-PQ and BT2020_RGB may describe native DisplayID signal support even without
-CTA metadata reception. Type 1 remains false and desired-content luminance
-remains zero. Native brightness, DCI-P3, HLG and other native color encodings
-are not guessed or synthesized. Unknown native enum values are retained
-privately, diagnosed and excluded from capability mapping. Known valid native
-fields survive unrelated reserved values.
-
-There is a narrow, separately reviewed malformed-input difference from
-upstream 4adfd7888e03987a4904a4ce0e14fd0ff4bccc42. Unusable nested CTA evidence
-suppresses only native fallback; it never clears valid parsed CTA fields.
-The marker is set for an unsupported 0x81 revision/reserved header flag,
-an incomplete recognized 0x81 header, a declared outer length beyond the
-section, a truncated nested child,
-an empty extended tag, or a rejected HDR/colorimetry child. Unsupported parent
-formats are skipped wholly. For a supported parent with a malformed tail,
-earlier complete children remain authoritative, including their Type 1 and
-luminance. Valid empty or irrelevant/audio-only nested CTA does not suppress
-native fallback. Unrelated diagnostics and malformed native blocks do not
-set this marker. Allocation failures are fatal and remain ENOMEM.
-
-The backport adapts upstream Simon Ser's private CTA context and DisplayID2
-lifecycle changes d535192, 34b3635, 4713505, 788c056, 7324cca and 73ec53d, plus
-the locally reviewed parser/safety/fallback commits da6168b, 533a338 and
-4adfd78. The conservative malformed-input marker and ownership repairs are
-local adaptations, not fabricated upstream commits or accepted patches.
-
-This is an unsigned local test build, not an Ubuntu upload or an upstream
-acceptance claim. Human DCO attestation, normative DisplayID section checking
-and maintainer review of the high-level semantics remain submission gates.
-The private observed panel capture is not included in the source package.
-
-Allocation-failure tests use a private static source copy so linker wrapping
-actually reaches nested allocations. Those test objects and the allocation
-executable disable LTO, preserving optimization and hardening. Whole-program
-analysis cannot observe linker --wrap counter side effects; the O2/LTO build
-elided a required counter reload after cleanup. The installed library and
-old-header ABI consumer retain production LTO. No assertion is weakened.
+This coordinated-stack prerequisite adds four upstream-proposed parsed-block
+getters and their native Display Interface Features types to libdisplay-info
+0.3. The new functions retain upstream ownership/lifetime semantics. Existing
+public types retain their layouts and the SONAME remains .so.3; pkg-config
+version remains the truthful 0.3.0. The development package requires its exact
+matching runtime, and the added symbols require 0.3.0-1ubuntu1~hdr4 or newer.
+
+Legacy HDR-static-metadata and supported-signal-colorimetry getters do not
+promote native-only DisplayID support: PQ, Type 1 and signal colorimetry stay
+false, with unknown desired-content nits zero. This restores the native-only
+contract after the superseded hdr3 experiment. The previous backport's nested
+CTA lookup is retained intentionally: ordinary and nested declarations join
+in extension/block order, first match per CTA domain remains authoritative.
+That nested behavior extends original Ubuntu 0.3 and is not claimed identical
+to its ordinary-only lookup. Complete CTA Type 1 and luminance remain intact.
+
+An explicit new consumer may use the parsed native API for a complete RGB
+BT.2020/PQ tuple in one block. Mutter's coordinated candidate does so only
+for eligible eDP/KMS/device support, preserving CTA authority and rejecting
+diagnosed inputs. No caller is silently migrated through the old getters.
+Unsupported nested CTA parent revisions/header flags are diagnosed and skipped;
+truncation and malformed children remain diagnosed, with complete earlier
+children preserved. Valid empty/audio-only containers remain usable.
+
+This library package alone does not enable desktop HDR. The coordinated
+Mutter opt-in and validated native luminance Intel display kernel route must
+be qualified together. Old-kernel/new-Mutter native HDR is not supported by
+this bounded stack until separately tested. No Type 1/content metadata is
+fabricated, and native panel brightness is not mastering/content metadata.
+
+The source preserves the attributed historical CTA/lifecycle backports and
+local parser changes identified in the quilt header. The additive API derives
+from the locally reviewed, unpushed parser proposal 533a338, not an accepted
+upstream release. Human DCO, normative section verification, maintainer review
+and any public submission remain separate gates.
+
+Fault tests use a private static parser copy and effective linker wrapping.
+Only that copy and the allocation executable disable LTO; optimization,
+hardening, all ownership/failure assertions and production library LTO remain.
+The private observed panel capture is excluded from source/package payloads.
